# THE VERSE-DEV DRIVE: the charter staging runs.
#
# Two halves, four strata and eight rules, in one file because they are one program: the REVIEW
# reads main's tip against itself and remembers what it found, and the MERGE GATE turns what the
# review's fixes deliver into a proposal somebody decides. Install it whole —
#
#     verse trigger add --file docs/working/comment-drift.tony
#
# — which installs the strata first whatever order they appear in (cmd/verse: "STRATA FIRST"),
# so the ordering is in force before a rule names it.
#
# IT WAS TWO FILES ON TWO BRANCHES, and that is worth a sentence because it is the failure this
# fixes rather than a tidying. The review half lived only on `use/verse-dev` and the merge half
# only on `main`, so nothing anywhere held the charter staging was actually running: reseeding
# meant dumping the store and trusting the dump, and scripts/staging-reseed.sh said so in as many
# words. One file on main, and a wipe costs nothing that is not written down.
#
# The two halves keep their own numbering — the review's rules are 1..3, the gate's are 1..3 with
# a 1b apiece — because the prose in each refers to its own by number.

# ── THE REVIEW ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# verse-dev drive: `main` moves, and the comments on every Go symbol the commit touched get read
# against the code under them — the ones that disagree get fixed, on a branch, and what is left
# over is remembered.
#
#   docs/working/verse-dev/comment-drift-install.sh — on `use/verse-dev`, and it predates this
#   file: it removes the review's three rules and three strata before re-adding them, because
#   `verse trigger add` refuses what is already installed. Re-installing all eight needs the
#   same treatment for the gate's five and `merge`.
#
# THREE rules and three strata, where the obvious shape is one rule. Each of the three is a wall
# this drive walked into, and the note has them at length
# (docs/working/verse-dev-comment-drift.md):
#
# ONE — a spawning action must take its subject out of its own selection, and a subject in a
# REFLECTED slice cannot hold the marker that would do it: a source is the single writer of what
# it reflects, so a latch written onto `git:*` is gone on the next pass that mentions the entity.
# Hence `head-moved`: the fact is copied into a slice this charter owns, and the spawn latches
# there — which is what buys the derive, and so the strata.
#
# TWO — a subject is a SELECTION, so a rule over `git:commit` is as wide as the reflected
# history: 131 commits on staging, all 131 firings timing out inside the store, every derive
# lost. `git:head:verse` is one entity carrying main's tip and it changes exactly when main
# moves. Width one, same event.
#
# THREE — the review used to re-audit whole files on every push, because nothing remembered what
# had already been checked: seventeen boundaries and forty-one shell calls to conclude that a
# 1500-line file was fine. So findings are FACTS now. `gather` reads the outstanding ones onto
# the subject before the task starts, the prompt carries them, and the answer becomes one entity
# per symbol (`!each`, docs/strata.md). A review reads its own memory and looks at what changed.

# The reflected slice: the git source's, and nobody else writes it.
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git

# Ours: the tip, as a fact this charter owns and latches on.
- name: drift
  level: 10
  match:
    system: drift
    kind: head

# The ledger. Strictly above `drift`, because that is the only direction a derive may go — and
# split by KIND rather than into another system, so the whole drive stays one slice a person can
# list with `verse entity --system drift`.
- name: ledger
  level: 20
  match:
    system: drift
    kind: finding
---
# 1. Main's tip, as a fact this charter owns — outstanding again the moment it moves.
#
# It spawns nothing, so it needs no latch: a derive's address is a function of (rule, subject),
# so a re-fire writes the same bytes to the same address and the store ends it as a no-op.
#
# `reviewed: pending` is the latch's other half, and it is a STATE rather than an absence for a
# reason that cost this drive an evening. The documented spelling of "not yet reviewed" is
# absence — `!not {reviewed: null}` — and verse cannot store one: `entity/logdstore.go` spells
# absence by WRITING a bare null and hiding every bare null it reads, at any depth, so the
# durable rule comes back as `!not {}` and selects nothing. The charter works until the first
# restart and then silently stops. (Not the substrate's doing: logd holds `b: null` across
# restart and compaction, and tony's patch sets null rather than deleting.) A state needs no
# null, and this one re-arms itself: a derive MERGES, so writing `pending` puts the fact back in
# rule 2's selection whenever the sha moves.
#
# The deletes are tagged operators rather than bare nulls, which is why THEY survive the same
# round trip: they clear the last review so a new one cannot merge with the one before it.
- name: head-moved
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject: git:head:verse
  action:
    derive:
      drift:
        head:
          repo: $subject.value.repo
          sha: $subject.value.sha
          reviewed: pending
          result: !delete null
          err: !delete null
          outstanding: !delete null
---
# 2. Before the review, the ledger is read onto the subject.
#
# A separate rule rather than a line in the next one, and the reason is ordering: a spawn's
# strings bind against the payload that FIRED the action, so an action that patched `outstanding`
# and then spawned would hand the task the payload from before its own write. Two firings, two
# commits, and the second one sees the first.
#
# It reads UPWARD — the findings are a stratum above — which a patch may do freely. Only a path
# read inside a DERIVE is confined to its own level or below, because that is the read that could
# make a derive's content depend on what the level above wrote.
#
# `!list-paths` with no `path:` yields whole payloads, so the prompt carries what each finding
# SAYS rather than a list of names. `status: open` is the narrowing, and it is also the human
# override: set a finding's status to anything else and it stops being handed to the agent, with
# no charter change and nothing to argue with.
- name: gather
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      system: drift
      kind: head
      value:
        reviewed: pending
  action:
    patch:
      reviewed: gathered
      outstanding: !list-paths
        system: drift
        kind: finding
        id: "*"
        value:
          status: open
---
# 3. …and the tip is reviewed, once, against what is already known.
#
# The latch is `reviewed: gathered`, which rule 2 writes and all three terminal hooks write past.
# A hook that did not would leave that ending unlatched, and the next thing to touch this entity
# would spawn again.
- name: comment-drift
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      system: drift
      kind: head
      value:
        reviewed: gathered
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: verse:runners:pool:code
      # That environment pushes `refs/heads/verse/auto/$task`, so the deliverable is a diff
      # rather than prose about a diff. Delivery is the runner's act and the task only proposes
      # it, at the end; a run that changed nothing proposes nothing and leaves no branch. It is
      # also the only environment on this runner with a shell — see the ConfigMap for what that
      # buys and what it costs.
      environment: verse-fix
      driver: llm
      # `all` because there is no smaller name that permits delegation: `read-only` is reads
      # alone, and `no-delegate` is defined by excluding CallAgent. The floor that actually
      # bounds this task is the environment's tool list and bwrap, not this word.
      grant: all
      # The firing answered its own gates — it has none — so the task does not re-ask per
      # action. Nothing here reaches outside the sandbox.
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        Commit $sha is the tip of main on the verse repository, checked out at ./verse with its
        full history. Review THAT commit, and answer with `at: $sha` — the tip can move while you
        work, so your answer has to say which commit it is about rather than leaving a reader to
        assume it was the latest.

        WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN. These findings are outstanding from earlier reviews. Each is a
        claim that some Go symbol's comments disagree with its code, and each is either still
        true or fixed by what has landed since:

        $outstanding

        WHAT CHANGED. The Go files this commit touched:

            git -C verse show --name-only --format= $sha -- '*.go'

        YOU DO NOT READ SOURCE FILES YOURSELF. Your whole job is to list what changed, hand each
        file to one agent, and assemble what they answer. A file you open is a file being
        reviewed twice, by the one reader who cannot see the others.

        For EACH of those files, call_agent once — one agent per file, running concurrently.
        Give each of them the path, the outstanding findings naming a symbol in it, and this job:

            Read `git -C verse show $sha -- <path>` and the declarations it changed.

            Judge the comments on those declarations, and on any symbol an outstanding finding
            names in this file. A comment is STALE when it disagrees with the code it is
            attached to: a parameter that is gone, a rationale for a branch that was deleted, a
            "returns X" that returns Y, an algorithm described that was replaced under it.
            Terse, unfashionable and MISSING are not stale — staleness is a disagreement, not a
            gap.

            FIX each stale one in place with write_file: rewrite the comment to say what the
            code now does, in the voice of the comments around it. Change nothing else — not
            the code, not the formatting, not a comment that is merely unlovely. Run no git
            command that writes: the working tree is the handoff, and delivery is the runner's.

            WHAT YOU DO NOT DO, because the working tree IS the deliverable and every file you
            leave behind is committed: do not write a test, do not run one, do not build, and
            do not add a file. You are judging whether a comment disagrees with the code beside
            it — never whether the code is right. A test you write to satisfy yourself lands in
            somebody's branch as though a reviewer had asked for it.

            READ EACH THING ONCE. Re-reading a file you have read does not make a verdict
            truer, and the second read costs a turn the first one already bought. When every
            symbol you were given has a verdict, answer — a conclusion you have reached and are
            still checking is a conclusion you are spending money to keep.

            Report an entry ONLY for a symbol that has something to say. A symbol you judged
            and found ACCURATE is not an entry. Say the symbol, the status, what the comment
            gets wrong (or got wrong), `at: $sha`, and `proposedAt` per below.

            `status: resolved` IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE COMMIT YOU ARE REVIEWING, and only that:
            the comments were wrong before and are right in what you are reading now. Something
            landed.

            A FIX YOU JUST WROTE RESOLVES NOTHING. Your edits are in a working tree that becomes
            a branch, and a branch is a PROPOSAL — somebody has to approve it, and it can be
            declined, or superseded, or retired unmerged while main moves on. So a symbol you
            fixed is still `status: open`, because it is still wrong in the commit under review,
            and you say where you proposed the fix: `proposedAt: $sha`. The review that comes
            after the fix lands reads main, finds the comment accurate, and resolves it. That is
            what makes the ledger a record of MAIN rather than of what agents believe they did.

            This is not hypothetical bookkeeping. A review fixed `task.Fatals`, marked it
            resolved, and its branch was retired before the merge; main kept the wrong comment
            while the ledger said somebody had dealt with it, and nothing re-examined it,
            because a resolved finding is not handed to anybody again.

            A finding you are handed that already carries `proposedAt` and is STILL WRONG means
            that fix did not land. Fix it again and say `proposedAt: $sha` for this attempt: the
            work is cheap and idempotent, where a comment nobody corrects is permanent.

        When every agent has reported, ANSWER. Do not go and check their work: you dispatched
        one reader per file precisely so that no one reader has to hold all of it, and reading
        the files yourself to confirm them undoes that and costs the run twice over.

        Then answer for all of them together. `findings` is what CHANGED, not an inventory: an
        outstanding finding you did not touch stays outstanding on its own and will be handed to
        you again, so leaving it out is correct and re-listing it costs everyone tokens for no
        fact. What you must not do is mark one resolved on the strength of your own edits — that
        is the lie the next reader inherits, and it is the one the ledger cannot catch, because
        a resolved finding is never handed to anybody again.

        The two entries above are the two cases: the first was wrong and is right in the commit
        being reviewed, with no fix of yours behind it (`proposedAt: ""`); the second is still
        wrong there, and you have proposed a fix for it.

        Name each symbol the way Go does, repo-relative: `entity.nodeToPayload`,
        `source/github.Reflect`, `entity.(*LogdStore).Get`. That identity survives the comment
        being rewritten and the declaration moving between files, which a line number does not.
        `go doc ./<pkg> <Name>` says whether you have it right.

        YOUR FINAL MESSAGE IS THE ANSWER — a JSON document, nothing before it and nothing
        after it. JSON because it is the shape you are most reliable at, and because verse takes
        it everywhere an answer is taken: valid JSON is valid tony, so it parses on the way in.
        Every key and every string is quoted, which is the whole of what has gone wrong before.

            {
              "files": 2,
              "fixed": 1,
              "findings": [
                {"symbol": "entity.nodeToPayload",
                 "status": "resolved",
                 "says": "claimed logd spells a tombstone as a bare null; the convention is verse's",
                 "at": "$sha",
                 "proposedAt": ""},
                {"symbol": "source/github.Reflect",
                 "status": "open",
                 "says": "says it returns the live set; it returns the set plus a settled tail",
                 "at": "$sha",
                 "proposedAt": "$sha"}
              ],
              "report": "one paragraph on what you changed and why",
              "at": "$sha"
            }

        `"findings": []` is the ORDINARY answer, not a failure: a commit whose comments are all
        accurate has nothing to add to the ledger, and so is one that changed no Go file at all —
        `{"files": 0, "fixed": 0, "findings": [], "report": "…", "at": "$sha"}`.

      # Checked when the model stops. `findings` is what the fan-out below walks, so a task that
      # narrated instead of answering fails HERE, before any derive, rather than at patch time
      # with the tokens already spent.
      answer:
        files: !irtype 0
        fixed: !irtype 0
        # `!all` constrains EVERY element, which is the only way an element's shape is stated at
        # all — and it has to be stated here, because the derive below dereferences all four
        # fields. An element missing `says` fails the FIRING, after the reading and the edits are
        # done: one completed review lost its findings that way. In the contract, the same
        # omission is a repair the agent can still make.
        #
        # The exemplars are meaningful because they cost nothing to make so: `!irtype` reads only
        # the TYPE of what it is written with, so writing a real symbol name instead of "text"
        # makes the example the repair renders an instance somebody can copy.
        findings: !all
          symbol: !irtype "entity.nodeToPayload"
          status: !or [open, resolved]
          says: !irtype "what the comment claims, against what the code does"
          at: !irtype "9d007068dc826d6bd2df9e01f70bd601a2d33f7d"
          # WHERE A FIX WAS PROPOSED, and "" when none was. `!irtype` reads the type alone, so
          # one exemplar admits both — the field is required and its emptiness is the answer,
          # which is what keeps the derive below total.
          proposedAt: !irtype "9d007068dc826d6bd2df9e01f70bd601a2d33f7d"
        report: !irtype "one paragraph on what changed and why"
        # WHICH COMMIT this answer is about, and it is not redundant with the subject's `sha`.
        # The tip moves while a review runs: `head-moved` re-derives, the subject's sha becomes
        # the newer one, and a result landing afterwards sits on a fact naming a commit the task
        # never read. Observed on the first run of this shape — a report about 068cc5bb on an
        # entity saying feedeef6. The answer carrying its own sha is what makes the fact
        # self-describing rather than merely recent.
        at: !irtype "9d007068dc826d6bd2df9e01f70bd601a2d33f7d"
    onDone:
      patch:
        reviewed: done
        result: $data
        # Read from the ANSWER, not from the subject: `$subject.value` is not available in a
        # hook at all, and the whole point is that the two can disagree.
        reviewedSha: $data.at
        err: !delete null
      # One entity per SYMBOL — not per comment. A claim about a symbol is re-checkable against
      # exactly one declaration, and it dissolves the sub-symbol case rather than extending it:
      # a comment on a loop is part of what the claim about that symbol says. Per-comment
      # identity would churn at the worst moment, since fixing a comment is what changes it.
      #
      # Append-only, deliberately (docs/strata.md): a finding this answer stopped mentioning
      # keeps its entity. Resolution is a fact somebody wrote — `status: resolved` here, or by
      # hand — never an absence inferred from a model going quiet.
      #
      # AND IT IS A FACT ABOUT MAIN. `resolved` means the comment is right in the commit that
      # was reviewed, not that an agent fixed it somewhere: a fix lives on a branch until it is
      # approved and merged, and branches are declined, superseded and retired. `proposedAt`
      # carries where the attempt was made, so a finding still open with a proposedAt behind it
      # is a fix that did not land — visible in the ledger rather than lost with the branch.
      derive:
        drift:
          finding: !each
            over: $data.findings
            key: $item.symbol
            value:
              symbol: $item.symbol
              status: $item.status
              says: $item.says
              at: $item.at
              proposedAt: $item.proposedAt
    onFailed:
      patch:
        reviewed: failed
        err: $err
        result: !delete null
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        reviewed: cancelled
        result: !delete null
        err: !delete null

---

# ── THE MERGE GATE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# verse-dev drive: an agent's branch is a PROPOSAL, and somebody decides it.
#
# The coding runner delivers to `verse/auto/<task>` (coderunner/workspace.go: that prefix is
# chosen so a developer who pulls and finds a commit nobody wrote can tell which system made it
# from the first two segments). Today those branches accumulate and nothing consumes them —
# three on this repository, all abandoned. This is the other half: the branch becomes a fact,
# the fact carries a gate, and a yes merges it.
#
# TWO STRATA AND FOUR RULES of its own, and the shape is borrowed wholesale from the review
# above, which paid for it. `reflected` is that half's, and this half has been moved in beside
# it — which is what the note here used to ask for, and why there is one `reflected` in this file
# rather than a second one the install would refuse.
#
# WHAT DECIDES THE DESIGN is staleness. A `verse/auto/*` branch is cut from whatever main was
# when its task started, so by the time anybody answers, main may have moved — and then
# `--ff-only` fails and something has to resolve a conflict nobody watched. The decision here is
# to REFUSE rather than resolve, and it is cheap precisely because the review that produced the
# branch is idempotent by construction: findings are facts, the ledger remembers, and a second
# pass reads its own memory and looks at what changed. Re-running a review costs a minute;
# rebasing an agent's work costs a second agent action and a second gate.
#
# THE MERGE RUNS NOW, AND THE SWEEP STILL DOES NOT. Both used to spawn a task that ran `git
# push`, and a task on the code runner could not reach a remote at all — which is what
# reference/examples/README.md now documents as landed.
#
# What landed: an environment declares DESTINATIONS by name, a task names one and never a ref,
# the vector gates the ref it resolves to, and approval is what releases the credential. So rule
# 2 below merges LOCALLY and then calls the runner's `push` tool naming `main`. Its prerequisite
# is in deploy/coderunner/coderunner.yaml, and it is a SEPARATE ENVIRONMENT rather than two more
# lines on `verse-fix`: `verse-merge` declares `main` and nothing else, offers `push`, and both
# rules below spawn into it. That matters because verse-fix is shared with comment-drift, which
# spawns with `vector: auto` — so had the destination gone there, what stopped a fix task pushing
# main would have been every charter remembering to narrow its own `tools:`. Split, a charter
# that has not been pointed at verse-merge cannot reach main however its vector is set.
#
# What did not: rule 4's DISCOVERY. Its delete landed (a destination declaring `delete: true`,
# which this rule names as `retire`) and its task need not be an LLM any more (a call list), but
# nothing here can yet say WHICH proposals are stale: that is `base` differing from a value held
# by another entity, and a match compares against literals. See merge-gate-residual.md.
#
# `--ff-only` stays a prompt-level instruction on purpose, backed by
# `receive.denyNonFastForwards` at the receiving end: `where` scopes onto a credential and `how`
# does not, so a model that ignores the instruction gets refused by the remote rather than
# merging some other way. That refusal is now an ANSWER rather than an error — the task completes
# and says it could not, which is what rule 2's `onDone` branches on.
#
# That decision buys two things worth noticing. The merge ATTEMPT is the staleness test — no
# comparison operator is needed anywhere, because `git merge --ff-only` either works or does
# not, and rule 2's `onFailed` is what "it went stale under us" means. And the sweep below needs
# no clock: a branch's mergeability changes exactly when main moves, and main moving is already
# an event this verse reflects. A periodic check would be asking a question whose answer only
# changes on an edge we already have.

# Ours: one entity per delivered branch, and one for the sweep. Strictly above `reflected`,
# because that is the only direction a derive may go.
# LEVEL 30, not 10, and the number is the one thing here that is not free to choose. The review
# above holds 0/10/20 (reflected, drift, ledger), and a level is the ordering — two strata at one level do not say which comes first, so installing
# them together is REFUSED until one moves. Found by installing it: `stratum "merge" wants level
# 10, which "drift" already holds`.
- name: merge
  level: 30
  match:
    system: merge
---
# 1. A delivered branch becomes a proposal, with the tip it was cut against RECORDED.
#
# `base` is the whole design. `!get-path` copies and does not track — read once, at the commit
# that caused the firing — which is exactly what is wanted here and would be a defect anywhere
# else: it freezes what main was when this branch appeared, so the proposal carries its own
# answer to "could this still fast-forward" without anything having to watch.
#
# It spawns nothing, so it needs no latch: a derive's address is a function of (rule, subject),
# so a re-fire writes the same bytes to the same address and the store ends it as a no-op. A
# force-push to the branch moves `sha`, re-fires this, and re-reads `base` — which is right, a
# redelivered branch is a new proposal.
- name: proposal-appeared
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      system: git
      kind: ref
      value:
        # TWO segments, not one: the runner's push target is `refs/heads/verse/auto/$sha/$task`
        # and `!glob` is filepath.Match, where `*` does NOT cross `/`. Written as
        # `verse/auto/*` this matched nothing at all — the charter predates the `$sha` segment,
        # and a selector that silently selects nothing is the failure this whole drive keeps
        # finding. Verified against a real delivered branch.
        name: !glob "verse/auto/*/*"
  action:
    # FACTS ONLY, and that is the whole of the rule this got wrong. A derive MERGES its literal
    # fields every time it runs — which this charter relies on elsewhere — so any field named
    # here is restated on every re-derive. `state: open` dragged a merged proposal back to
    # undecided and `base` tracked main's current tip, which is the left side of the staleness
    # test comparing itself against the thing it is compared to.
    #
    # What is left is a function of the subject alone, so re-deriving cannot change an answer.
    # `base` is pinned once by proposal-based below; `state` is written only by the rules that
    # decide, and its ABSENCE is what undecided means.
    derive:
      merge:
        proposal:
          repo: $subject.value.repo
          branch: $subject.value.name
          sha: $subject.value.sha
---
# 1b. …and the commit it was cut from is pinned, once.
#
# `base` reads main's tip, so it is time-dependent and cannot come from a derive that re-runs.
# This is the latch the rest of the charter runs on, applied to a field: it selects the proposals
# nobody has pinned, and writing one takes that proposal out of its own selection. The derive
# above never names `base`, so nothing restates it afterwards.
#
# A proposal is briefly baseless — between the derive and this — which is why every rule that
# compares against `base` asks for it by comparing, and a comparison against an absent field does
# not match.
- name: proposal-based
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      system: merge
      kind: proposal
      value: !not.has-path base
  action:
    patch:
      base: !get-path { system: git, kind: head, id: verse, path: sha }
---
# 2. …and somebody decides it.
#
# The gate carries a formula rather than waiting on a person indefinitely, and that is not
# ceremony: a question whose only discharge is somebody choosing to look is the wait
# `task/contract.go` property 5 forbids one level down, and it does not become acceptable by
# moving up a layer. `!until [!quorum 1, !deadline "24h"]` is the tutorial's documented shape and
# it defaults to DENY when the clock runs out, which is the safe direction for the one action in
# this repository with a consequence nobody can quietly undo.
#
# (A contract notice wants the INVERSE — carry on the clock, an objection interrupts — which is
# verse-dev step 2's open question. A merge is the case the documented form was written for, so
# it gets it, and the two genuinely differ rather than one being a bug.)
#
# `onDenied` is required by what a deny does: a refused firing writes nothing, so without this
# the subject is left in the exact state that asked, and the next commit re-poses the same
# question to somebody who already answered it.
#
# The task's own vector is `auto`. The decision has been made HERE, by a person, about the thing
# that matters; making the task re-ask per action would be asking the same question twice in two
# vocabularies.
- name: merge-proposal
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      system: merge
      kind: proposal
      # ONLY WHILE IT CAN STILL LAND. A branch fast-forwards onto main exactly while main is
      # where it was cut from, so `base == tip` is the same predicate rule 3 uses negated —
      # and asking it HERE is what lets the task below be a call list.
      #
      # It used to be the task's job: an agent ran `--ff-only`, was refused, and reported
      # `merged: false` through an answer contract, because "main moved" is the ORDINARY
      # outcome of this gate and routing it through failure would have lost the difference
      # between that and a credential expiring. A call list has no answer to say it with. So
      # the charter asks the question instead, and what is left for the task to fail at is
      # infrastructure — which is what failure should mean.
      value: !and
      - !not.has-path state
      - !let
        let: [{tip: !get-path {system: git, kind: head, id: verse, path: sha}}]
        in: {base: .[tip]}
  gates:
  - name: merge-to-main
    mode: ask
    formula: !until [ !quorum 1, !deadline "24h" ]
    onDenied:
      patch:
        state: declined
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: verse:runners:pool:code
      environment: verse-merge
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      # Four git commands were what this always was. The model was rediscovering them every
      # firing, and when the branch went out from under it — retired by rule 3 while its motion
      # was open — it went looking for the objects on the filesystem instead of stopping.
      #
      # No `fatal:` on either call: the default is that anything ends the list, which is what
      # is wanted. If the merge does not fast-forward the push must not happen, and the whole
      # reason it might is now selected against above.
      calls:
      # THE MIRROR, not origin, and no fetch at all. The task's clone is made FROM the runner's
      # local mirror (`RemoteName: "mirror"`), which the runner refreshes when it opens the
      # workspace — so every delivered branch is already here as a remote-tracking ref, and
      # `origin` is a config entry pointing at a forge this sandbox has no route to fetch from.
      #
      # `fetch origin` is what the PROMPT said when this was an agent, and the agent quietly
      # adapted: it read the failure and used what was there. A call list cannot, which is the
      # trade — it does exactly what it is told, so what it is told has to be true. This one cost
      # a `exited 128` and is the clearest argument in the charter for why the list is worth it:
      # the instruction was wrong for as long as a model was papering over it.
      - tool: run_bash
        input:
          command: "git -C verse merge --ff-only refs/remotes/mirror/$branch"
      - tool: push
        input: {destination: main}
    onDone:
      patch:
        state: merged
        err: !delete null
    # What is left to fail is infrastructure, or main moving between this firing and the push —
    # which the remote refuses with `denyNonFastForwards` rather than accepting quietly.
    #
    # It lands on `failed` and STAYS there. Undecided is the absence of this field, and a patch
    # cannot unwrite one — `!delete` would, and using it here would put the proposal back in
    # front of the same gate on the next commit, asking a person to answer again for a run that
    # broke rather than for a decision that changed. A failure is a fact somebody looks at.
    onFailed:
      patch:
        state: failed
        err: $err
    # A cancel decided NOTHING, so the proposal goes back to undecided — which is now the
    # absence of the field, and `!delete` is what unwrites one. This is the case that earns the
    # operator: somebody stopped the run, so the gate should be asked again, where a FAILED run
    # should not re-ask anybody anything.
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        state: !delete null
        err: !delete null
---
# 1b. …and the proposal says what it is about, so the docket can ask a real question.
#
# WITHOUT THIS a person is asked `whether open` about
# `merge:proposal:proposal-appeared@git:ref:verse_verse_auto_…` and nothing else. To answer they
# must fetch the subject, read a branch name and two shas out of it, and go find the diff
# somewhere else — which is a gate answered by reflex, and the gate is the load-bearing part of
# this whole design. `verse docket` shows a subject's `says` when it has one, so putting the
# review's own report there is the whole fix.
#
# A PATCH rather than part of rule 1's derive, and the engine is what taught me: a derive may
# read at or below its own level and no higher, because reading upward lets a level above change
# what a derive writes and a derive that writes something new every lap wakes that level again.
# Rule 1 lives in `reflected` (0) because its subject is a git ref; the report lives in `drift`
# (10). So the read happens HERE, in `merge` (30), where it is a read downward and a patch —
# which the refusal itself points at: "A `patch` may read it".
#
# It needs no latch: patching the same value is a no-op the store ends, so this converges after
# one lap. And it races rule 2 harmlessly — the docket reads the subject when it RENDERS, not
# when the motion opens, so a `says` that lands a moment later is still there when somebody looks.
- name: proposal-says
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      system: merge
      kind: proposal
      value: !not.has-path state
  action:
    patch:
      says: !get-path { system: drift, kind: head, id: "head-moved@git:head:verse", path: result.report }
---
# 2b, 2c ARE GONE, and their absence is the point.
#
# They read the merge task's ANSWER — `decided` plus `merged: true|false` — and turned it into
# a state, because a patch derefs and does not branch. With the merge as a call list there is
# no answer to read: the task either did it or did not, so `onDone` writes `merged` and
# `onFailed` writes `open`, and the intermediate `decided` state nobody could act on stops
# existing. Two rules and one state, deleted by a driver change.
#
# (Their prose sits above rule 3 rather than in a document of its own: a `---` section holding
# only comments is an EMPTY DOCUMENT, and the parser refuses one — which is why this file could
# not be installed whole while these two paragraphs stood apart.)
#
# 3. Main moved, so the proposals cut from where it used to be can no longer land — and each
# one's branch is retired.
#
# TWO RULES COLLAPSED INTO THIS ONE. There used to be a `sweep-armed` rule whose whole job was to
# re-arm a `merge:sweep` entity every time the sha moved, and a `sweep` task that fetched the
# remote, ran `merge-base --is-ancestor` per branch and decided. Neither is needed: the charter
# already knows, because a proposal records the `base` it was cut from and main moving IS the
# staleness test. `base != <main's tip>` is the whole predicate — no ancestry walk, no remote
# asked anything, no model.
#
# What made it sayable is a condition that can read another entity. `!get-path` reads main's tip
# from the same snapshot this condition is evaluated against, `!let` binds it, and `!not` compares
# the subject's own field against it. The read joins what this trigger READS, so main moving
# re-evaluates the rule — which is the level-triggering the old rule 3 was hand-rolling with a
# derive.
#
# One firing per stale proposal, each with its own branch, so the retirement is one call and not
# a task that has to discover anything. `driver: calls` is implied by `calls:` itself.
#
# Ungated, deliberately, and it is the one call here worth arguing with. Deleting a
# `verse/auto/*` branch destroys nothing that cannot be remade: the work was proposed by a review
# that is idempotent by construction, and a branch that cannot fast-forward is a proposal about a
# main that no longer exists. Against that, a gate on every stale branch fills the docket with
# questions whose only honest answer is yes, and a docket nobody can keep up with is one nobody
# reads — which is the failure the whole gate mechanism is for.
#
# What it still cannot see is a question standing on the proposal it is retiring; see
# merge-gate-residual.md.
- name: proposal-outrun
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      system: merge
      kind: proposal
      value: !and
      - !not.has-path state
      - !let
        let: [{tip: !get-path {system: git, kind: head, id: verse, path: sha}}]
        in: {base: !not .[tip]}
  action:
    patch:
      state: stale
    spawn:
      runner: verse:runners:pool:code
      environment: verse-merge
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      calls:
      - tool: push
        input: {destination: retire, ref: $branch}
      # No `answer:`, and that is the driver rather than an omission: a call list has no model
      # to repair a contract with, so a spawn carrying one is refused. What used to be read out
      # of the answer — which branches went — is the LIST ITSELF, and the ref is in the record
      # before the task starts.
    #
    # The patch above already wrote `stale`, so a failed retirement lands on `stale` with an
    # `err` beside it: the proposal IS stale whether or not its branch could be removed, and the
    # branch outliving it is a ref nobody reads rather than a decision anybody is waiting on.
    onFailed:
      patch:
        err: $err
